## Proof

**Claim.** In any normed vector space $(E, N)$ (possibly infinite-dimensional), 
$$\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)=\{0\}.$$

**Proof.** The inclusion $\{0\}\subseteq\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)$ is immediate since every linear functional vanishes at $0$. We prove the reverse inclusion.

Let $x\in E$ with $x\neq 0$. We construct a continuous linear functional $f\in E'$ with $f(x)\neq 0$, which shows $x\notin\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)$.

Consider the one-dimensional subspace $G=\operatorname{span}\{x\}=\{\alpha x:\alpha\in\mathbb K\}$. Define $g:G\to\mathbb K$ by
$$g(\alpha x)=\alpha\, N(x).$$
This is well-defined because $x\neq 0$ makes the representation $\alpha x$ unique. It is linear, and for every $\alpha\in\mathbb K$,
$$|g(\alpha x)|=|\alpha|\,N(x)=N(\alpha x),$$
so $g$ is continuous on $G$ with operator norm $\|g\|=N(x)>0$ (in fact $\|g\|=1$ after normalizing, but the value $g(x)=N(x)\neq 0$ is what matters).

By the **Hahn–Banach theorem** (analytic form, valid in arbitrary normed spaces — no finite-dimensionality is required), $g$ extends to a continuous linear functional $f:E\to\mathbb K$ with $\|f\|=\|g\|$. In particular $f\in E'$ and
$$f(x)=g(x)=N(x)\neq 0.$$

Hence $x\notin\operatorname{Ker}(f)\supseteq\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)$ is false — more precisely, $x\notin\operatorname{Ker}(f)$, so $x\notin\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)$.

Since this holds for every $x\neq 0$, we conclude
$$\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)\subseteq\{0\}.$$

Combining both inclusions,
$$\boxed{\bigcap_{f\in E'}\operatorname{Ker}(f)=\{0\}.}$$

**Remark on the role of Hahn–Banach.** The key point is that the separating functional is built on the 1-dimensional subspace $\operatorname{span}\{x\}$ (where everything is elementary) and then extended to all of $E$ without increasing its norm. Hahn–Banach requires only the sublinear function $N$ (a norm is in particular a sublinear functional) and makes no assumption on $\dim E$, so the argument is valid in the infinite-dimensional case as well. Without Hahn–Banach the statement can fail for general locally convex topologies, but for the norm topology it always holds.

